     Passing the thread identifier of the calling thread (see thr_self(2)) to
     tthhrr__wwaakkee() sets a thread's flag to cause the next signal-interruptible
     sleep of that thread in the kernel to fail immediately with the EINTR er‐
     ror.  The flag is cleared by an interruptible sleep attempt or by a call
     to thr_suspend(2).  This is used by the system threading library to im‐
     plement cancellation.

     If _i_d is not equal to the current thread identifier, the specified thread
     is woken up if suspended by the thr_suspend(2) system call.  If the
     thread is not suspended at the time of the tthhrr__wwaakkee call, the wake is re‐
     membered and the next attempt of the thread to suspend itself with the
     thr_suspend(2) results in immediate return with success.  Only one wake
     is remembered.

RREETTUURRNN VVAALLUUEESS
     The tthhrr__wwaakkee() function returns the value 0 if successful; otherwise the
     value -1 is returned and the global variable _e_r_r_n_o is set to indicate the
     error.

EERRRROORRSS
     The tthhrr__wwaakkee() operation returns these errors:

     [ESRCH]            The specified thread was not found or does not belong
                        to the process of the calling thread.

